  "tldr": "The paper determines the minimax fixed-\\(\\alpha\\) expected length of connected honest confidence intervals for fuzzy-RD LATE when the compliance jump \\(\\kappa_n\\) and its unknown cutoff vanish in statistical visibility together. A causal location-mixture subexperiment yields a nonshrinking lower bound below \\(T_n=n\\kappa_n^{2+1/s}\\asymp\\log(1/\\kappa_n)\\), even when the known-cutoff interval shrinks and even when outcomes are observed. A scan--refine--invert interval recovers the known-cutoff order above this boundary. The central open theorem evaluates and matches the fixed-level length in the critical window by combining sharp compensation profiles, truncated location mixtures, and effect-label testing.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Weak fuzzy-RD inference is honest at a known cutoff, and unknown-cutoff theory localizes directly observed fixed or shrinking jumps, but neither literature characterizes connected LATE-interval length when the first-stage denominator and its location become weak jointly.",
    "niche": "The joint experiment has a nonconvex location union, a causal outcome--treatment coupling, and a fixed-level decision loss; its critical window cannot be read from a point-estimation cutoff rate or a known-cutoff weak-instrument inversion.",
    "fill": "The project embeds an outcome-invariant latent monotone packing in the published fuzzy-RD class, derives a finite-sample connected-length converse, constructs a four-fold bias-aware scan--invert interval, and evaluates the remaining critical-window experiment with an explicit attainment result."
  },
  "related_work": "Noack and Rothe (2024) motivate the setup: fuzzy-RD LATE is a ratio, weak compliance defeats ordinary Wald inference, and bias-aware Anderson--Rubin inversion restores known-cutoff honesty. Porter and Yu (2015) organize unknown-cutoff RD into detection, cutoff estimation, and effect estimation, but their oracle substitution argument relies on a fixed discontinuity and centers on sharp RD. M{\\\"u}ller and Song (1997) derive a triangular-drift Brownian localization limit for a vanishing regression jump, while Tuvaandorj (2020) obtains white-noise equivalence and minimax location rates under a jump floor; these results inform the local cutoff coordinate rather than the causal interval-length claim. Armstrong and Koles{\\'a}r (2018) supply the ordered-modulus and test-inversion template for fixed-level expected length on convex regression classes. Frick, Munk, and Sieling (2014) supply the multiscale scan architecture for step signals, and Chernozhukov, Chetverikov, and Kato (2014) supply Gaussian coupling and anti-concentration for the cutoff--scale maximum. Madrid Padilla, Yu, Wang, and Rinaldo (2022) show how aligned multivariate change channels affect localization, but their signal vectors are unrestricted rather than constrained by a latent monotone causal model. Kowalska, van de Wiel, and van der Pas (2025 version) jointly model fuzzy effects and an unknown cutoff in a Bayesian specification without a frequentist honest minimax frontier. Arai, Otsu, and Seo (2026) treat fuzzy and unknown-discontinuity extensions separately, leaving the joint weak-cutoff object untouched.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The displayed quartic family certifies the logarithmic barrier but is not asserted to solve the normalized compensation problem. The untruncated second-moment expression loses sharpness when \\(n\\kappa_n^{2+1/s}\\) is comparable to \\(\\log(1/\\kappa_n)\\); boundary truncation, mixture renormalization, and effect-label testing must be retained in the critical-window proof.",
  "honest_scope": "The finite-sample location barrier, the known-cutoff order, and the strong-signal scan--invert claim are supporting results to be proved. The exact fixed-\\(\\alpha\\) critical-window value and its attaining connected interval are deliberately exposed as Open-ended Question 1. At a supplied cutoff the proposal reduces to the known-cutoff weak-fuzzy-RD order, and in the fixed-jump face the scan--refine step reduces to the oracle unknown-cutoff logic of Porter and Yu (2015). No claim is made about disconnected confidence-set optimality, adaptation to unknown \\(s\\), or Bayesian credible-set calibration.",
